# radroots_runtime_paths

`radroots_runtime_paths` provides canonical, typed runtime paths for Radroots
core libraries. Its public API is exposed only from the crate root. Pure path
resolution never reads the ambient process environment or performs filesystem
I/O. State-directory provisioning is a separate explicit operation.

## Overview

- Validated service and instance identifiers define the sole canonical
  `services/<service>/<instance>` namespace.
- Bounded service, instance, and credential artifact names are validated as
  borrowed UTF-8 before the crate creates their retained strings; Serde identity
  decoding uses the same boundary without a second prevalidation copy.
- Callers inject the platform, host-environment roots, profile, and typed
  bootstrap provenance.
- A sealed `RuntimeContext` is the only public service-instance path
  construction boundary.
- A sealed state-directory plan validates or provisions only the canonical
  `services/<service>/<instance>` suffix under an existing state root.
- Service paths and common artifacts are non-forgeable immutable views.
- Pure shared geonames and runtime-store helpers remain independent of
  service-instance path selection.

The former generic app/service/worker/shared namespace, raw root containers,
path overrides, process-environment selectors, and bootstrap path helpers are
intentionally removed. Callers must construct typed bootstrap provenance and
resolve a `RuntimeContext` from an explicitly injected resolver.

## Example

The following deterministic Linux service-host example resolves one Myc
instance and its common artifacts without consulting or changing the host:

```rust
use std::path::Path;

use radroots_runtime_paths::{
    InstanceId, RadrootsHostEnvironment, RadrootsPathProfile,
    RadrootsPathResolver, RadrootsPlatform, RuntimeContext,
    RuntimeContextBootstrap, RuntimeContextSource, ServiceId,
    default_service_instance_artifacts,
};

let resolver = RadrootsPathResolver::new(
    RadrootsPlatform::Linux,
    RadrootsHostEnvironment::default(),
);
let bootstrap = RuntimeContextBootstrap::new(
    RadrootsPathProfile::ServiceHost,
    None,
    RuntimeContextSource::SafeDefault,
    RuntimeContextSource::BootstrapCli,
)?;
let context = RuntimeContext::resolve(
    &resolver,
    bootstrap,
    ServiceId::new("myc")?,
    InstanceId::new("primary")?,
)?;
let artifacts = default_service_instance_artifacts(context.paths());

assert_eq!(
    context.paths().config(),
    Path::new("/etc/radroots/services/myc/primary"),
);
assert_eq!(
    artifacts.config(),
    Path::new("/etc/radroots/services/myc/primary/config.toml"),
);
# Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>(())
```

## Root Profiles

Every root below receives the suffix `services/<service>/<instance>`. The
service instance's `state` directory is derived from the selected data root.

| Profile | Config root | Data/state root | Cache root | Logs root | Run root | Secrets root |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linux `ServiceHost` | `/etc/radroots` | `/var/lib/radroots` | `/var/cache/radroots` | `/var/log/radroots` | `/run/radroots` | `/etc/radroots/secrets` |
| Linux `InteractiveUser` | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radroots` | `$XDG_DATA_HOME/radroots` | `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/radroots` | `$XDG_STATE_HOME/radroots/logs` | `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/radroots` | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radroots/secrets` |
| macOS `InteractiveUser` | `$HOME/Library/Application Support/Radroots/config` | `$HOME/Library/Application Support/Radroots/data` | `$HOME/Library/Caches/Radroots` | `$HOME/Library/Logs/Radroots` | `$HOME/Library/Application Support/Radroots/run` | `$HOME/Library/Application Support/Radroots/secrets` |
| Windows `InteractiveUser` | `%APPDATA%\Radroots\config` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Radroots\data` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Radroots\cache` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Radroots\logs` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Radroots\run` | `%APPDATA%\Radroots\secrets` |
| `RepoLocal` | `<base>/config` | `<base>/data` | `<base>/cache` | `<base>/logs` | `<base>/run` | `<base>/secrets` |

Linux XDG inputs must be absolute. Empty, missing, or relative config, data,
state, and cache values use the corresponding absolute `HOME` defaults:
`.config`, `.local/share`, `.local/state`, and `.cache`. An absolute
`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is mandatory and has no fallback. Windows interactive
resolution requires both injected `APPDATA` and `LOCALAPPDATA`. Repo-local
resolution requires one explicit absolute, non-root base with no parent
traversal. Repo-local never becomes an implicit or production fallback.

## State Directory Provisioning

`RuntimeContext::state_directory_plan` is pure and performs no filesystem I/O.
Its sealed result exposes no caller-selected path or mutable component. Calling
`RuntimeStateDirectoryPlan::provision` is an explicit filesystem operation.

For `InteractiveUser` and `RepoLocal`, the canonical data/state root must
already exist, be owned by the effective user, be owner-readable and
owner-searchable, and not be group- or other-writable. The provisioner may then
create only `services/<service>/<instance>`, one descriptor-relative component
at a time, with mode `0700`. It uses no-follow opens, validates every existing
directory, never changes an existing mode, and identity-checks any cleanup of
directories it created during a failed attempt. An entry whose identity cannot
be proven is preserved and the operation fails closed.

For `ServiceHost`, the entire state-directory suffix must already exist. The
plan performs validation only and never creates or permission-repairs service-
host directories. Provisioning is implemented only on Linux and macOS; other
targets fail with a stable path-free unsupported-platform classification.

## Common Artifacts

| Artifact | Canonical location within the instance |
| --- | --- |
| Configuration | `<config>/config.toml` |
| SQLite state | `<state>/state.sqlite` |
| SQLite writer lock | `<state>/state.lock` |
| Local admin socket | `<run>/admin.sock` |
| Credential artifact | `<secrets>/<validated-credential-name>` |

The crate derives these paths. Its explicit state-directory provisioner owns
only the narrow behavior described above; it does not create any common
artifact, configuration, cache, log, run, or secrets path. Callers own artifact
creation and its security policy. The shared geonames and runtime-store helpers
are pure joins over caller-supplied roots and do not select or alter a
`RuntimeContext`.

## Support Caveats

Deterministic path behavior is not a qualification claim. Linux service-host
on x86_64 and aarch64 is eligible for Tier 1 only after all release gates pass.
Linux and macOS interactive and explicit repo-local profiles on x86_64 and
aarch64 are developer-target behavior. Linux rootless OCI on x86_64 and
aarch64 is also only a target: it uses the Linux behavior with explicit mounts,
has no separate native path profile, and requires its own qualification gates.
macOS support does not imply launchd packaging, system-wide paths, or Linux
peer-credential equivalence. Windows interactive and repo-local derivation is
implemented but unsupported and carries no v1 support claim.

Non-Linux `ServiceHost`, `MobileNative`, Android/iOS/Other interactive,
mobile/browser/WASI service use, and every unlisted profile/platform pairing
are unsupported. Successful compilation or path derivation does not change a
target's qualification posture.

## Public API Baseline

The final reviewed root-only API is frozen in the
[runtime-paths API baseline](../../contracts/api_baselines/radroots_runtime_paths.txt)
and checked by the package boundary tests.

